{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command \"Get-Location; Get-ChildItem -Force -Name; Get-Command codegraph -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Source; Get-Content -LiteralPath 'C:\\\\Users\\\\Ikran\\\\.agents\\\\PLATFORMS.md' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue; Get-Content -LiteralPath 'C:\\\\Users\\\\Ikran\\\\.agents\\\\skills\\\\ticket-master\\\\SKILL.md'; Get-Content -LiteralPath 'C:\\\\Users\\\\Ikran\\\\.agents\\\\skills\\\\handoff-watch\\\\SKILL.md'\"","aggregated_output":"\r\nPath                                              \r\n----                                              \r\nC:\\Users\\Ikran\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\a62-probe\\codex3\r\napp.js\r\nindex.html\r\nC:\\Users\\Ikran\\AppData\\Local\\codegraph\\current\\bin\\codegraph.cmd\r\n---\r\nname: ticket-master\r\ndescription: >-\r\n  Serialised, Jira-style ticket IDs for AI coding chats â€” every user-reported bug\r\n  is B1, B2, B3â€¦, every feature F1, F2, F3â€¦, and every issue the agent itself\r\n  finds (runtime errors, analysis, review) A1, A2, A3â€¦, with sub-parts as B7a/B7b,\r\n  all recorded in a\r\n  single shared TICKETS.md at the repo root so nothing is forgotten across chats,\r\n  sessions, or teammates on the same branch. Use when the user reports a bug or\r\n  asks for a feature, says \"log this\", \"what's pending\", \"ticket\", \"B3\", \"F12\",\r\n  \"next ticket number\", \"mark it done\", \"A4\", \"list tickets\", \"list tickets\r\n  status\", or when starting work and you need to know what's already open. Also use before closing a session to sync statuses. It\r\n  allocates IDs by scanning the file (never reusing a number), keeps status as\r\n  pending/in-progress/done, and appends rather than renumbering so parallel\r\n  teammates don't conflict. NOT an issue tracker replacement and NOT for\r\n  syncing to real Jira/GitHub Issues. The open set is worked as a single\r\n  deterministic Workflow script â€” one agent per ticket, fanned out and verified in\r\n  one call â€” whenever several tickets are open at once or the user says \"ultracode\", \"work the board\", \"use a workflow\",\r\n  \"fan out agents\".\r\n---\r\n\r\n# ticket-master â€” serialised tickets, worked as a workflow\r\n\r\nOne file, `TICKETS.md`, at the repo root. It is the source of truth. Bugs are\r\n`B<n>`, features are `F<n>`, agent-found issues are `A<n>`, sub-parts are the\r\nparent ID plus a letter (`B7a`).\r\nNumbers are **never reused** and **never renumbered** â€” an ID means one thing\r\nforever, in commits, branch names, and chat.\r\n\r\n## The file\r\n\r\nCreate it if missing, with exactly this shape:\r\n\r\n```markdown\r\n# TICKETS\r\n\r\nLegend: `[ ]` pending Â· `[~]` in-progress Â· `[!]` blocked Â· `[>]` deferred Â· `[x]` done Â· `[-]` cancelled\r\nIDs are permanent â€” never reuse or renumber. Append new tickets at the end of\r\ntheir section.\r\n\r\n## Bugs\r\n\r\n- [x] B1 â€” Login redirect loops on expired session\r\n- [~] B2 â€” CSV export drops the last row\r\n  - [x] B2a â€” off-by-one in the writer\r\n  - [ ] B2b â€” add regression test\r\n- [!] B3 â€” Avatar upload 500s over 5MB (blocked: needs S3 creds from ops)\r\n\r\n## Features\r\n\r\n- [ ] F1 â€” Dark mode\r\n- [ ] F2 â€” Bulk delete in the table view\r\n\r\n## Agent-found\r\n\r\n- [ ] A1 â€” Unhandled promise rejection in the upload worker (runtime)\r\n- [>] A2 â€” `parseDate` silently returns Invalid Date on empty string (deferred: after the parser rewrite)\r\n```\r\n\r\nLine format, kept greppable: `- [<status>] <ID> â€” <one-line title>`. Optional\r\ntrailing ` (@owner)` or ` (branch: x)` if the user wants it; nothing else.\r\nSub-parts are indented two spaces under their parent.\r\n\r\n## Allocating an ID\r\n\r\n1. Read `TICKETS.md` (create it from the template above if absent).\r\n2. Next ID = highest existing number in that section + 1. Scan the *whole* file,\r\n   including done tickets â€” done never frees a number.\r\n3. On a shared branch, also check for uncommitted/incoming edits before\r\n   allocating: `git fetch && git diff HEAD origin/<branch> -- TICKETS.md`. If the\r\n   remote has higher numbers, take the next one above those.\r\n4. Sub-parts: next unused letter under that parent (`B2a`, `B2b`, â€¦). Use them\r\n   only when a ticket genuinely splits into separately-completable pieces.\r\n\r\n**Collision rule:** if two teammates ever land the same number, the later commit\r\nrenames its ticket to a fresh number and leaves ` (was B7)` on the line. Never\r\nrenumber the earlier one.\r\n\r\n## When to log\r\n\r\n- User reports a bug â†’ log a `B` ticket before fixing.\r\n- User asks for a feature/change of any size â†’ log an `F` ticket before building.\r\n- **Anything Claude/an agent finds itself â†’ an `A` ticket.** A stack trace in a\r\n  test run, a crash while driving the app, a bug spotted during review or\r\n  analysis, an unrelated problem hit mid-task: log it as pending and keep going.\r\n  `A` means \"nobody asked for this, we found it\" â€” it is what keeps discoveries\r\n  from dying with the chat.\r\n\r\nTrivial one-line edits the user asked for inline don't need a ticket. If in\r\ndoubt, log it â€” a pending line is cheap.\r\n\r\n## Working a ticket\r\n\r\n- Starting â†’ flip to `[~]`.\r\n- Finished *and verified* (tests/build/manual check actually run) â†’ `[x]`.\r\n  Not verified, not done.\r\n- Can't proceed â€” waiting on someone, a credential, an upstream fix, or another\r\n  ticket â†’ `[!]`, with ` (blocked: <what on>)` on the line. Blocked is still\r\n  **open**: report it at session end, and unblock back to `[ ]` or `[~]` the\r\n  moment the dependency clears. Never leave an agent spinning on a blocked\r\n  ticket â€” flip it and move to the next one.\r\n- Postponed by choice â€” not now, waiting on a condition that isn't a hard\r\n  dependency (a later milestone, a build that hasn't run, hardware not on the\r\n  bench) â†’ `[>]`, with ` (deferred: <until what>)` on the line. Deferred is\r\n  **not open and not closed** â€” it is off the board until the condition\r\n  arrives: not pending (nobody is picking it up next), not blocked (nobody owes\r\n  you anything), not cancelled (it will be done). Report it at session end as\r\n  its own line, and flip it back to `[ ]` when the condition arrives.\r\n- Dropped â€” won't fix, obsolete, duplicate, or the user called it off â†’ `[-]`,\r\n  with a short ` â€” reason` on the line (`[-] B9 â€” Safari flicker (dup of B4)`).\r\n  Cancelled is a closed state, not a deleted one: the line and its number stay.\r\n- Reference the ID in commit messages: `B3: reject uploads over 5MB`.\r\n- Cycle end / session end: re-read the file and correct any status that drifted,\r\n  then report the open set (`[ ]` and `[~]`) to the user.\r\n\r\n## Work tickets in parallel, not in a line\r\n\r\nFlipping a ticket to `[~]` means **dispatch an agent for it**, one agent per\r\nticket, all in a single message so they run concurrently. Never work a queue of\r\ntickets serially in the main session â€” that is the linear development this skill\r\nexists to break.\r\n\r\n- Give each agent its ticket ID, the one-line title, and the repo context it\r\n  needs; tell it to report back what it changed and whether it verified.\r\n- Only the main session edits `TICKETS.md` â€” agents report, you flip statuses.\r\n  Two agents writing the same file is how you lose tickets.\r\n- Tickets touching the same files are the one exception: run those sequentially,\r\n  or hand the whole cluster to one agent, and say so.\r\n- Sub-parts (`B2a`, `B2b`) are the unit to parallelise when a ticket is big.\r\n\r\n## Workflow mode â€” working the board in one call\r\n\r\nDynamic workflows let you write a script that orchestrates many agents\r\ndeterministically: real control flow, structured results, a resumable run. Same\r\nboard as plain dispatch, scripted.\r\n\r\n**Switch to workflow mode when any of these hold:**\r\n\r\n- **4+ open tickets** to work in this cycle (real fan-out).\r\n- The tickets are a sweep â€” the same change across N files, an audit, a codemod.\r\n- You want each fix **adversarially verified** before flipping it to `[x]`.\r\n- The user asked: **\"ultracode\"**, \"use a workflow\", \"work the board\", \"fan out\r\n  agents\", \"orchestrate this with subagents\".\r\n\r\n**Stay with plain dispatch when:** 1-3 tickets, tickets needing your judgement\r\nbetween them, or a host with no `Workflow` tool. Off `claude-code`, workflow mode\r\nis a *pattern* â€” emulate it with the host's parallel delegate mechanism and don't\r\nclaim a `Workflow` call happened. See `WORKFLOW.md` beside the installed skills\r\nfor the host table.\r\n\r\n**Never start a workflow the user didn't opt into.** A workflow can spawn dozens\r\nof agents; if the board is big and they didn't ask, say what it would cost first.\r\n\r\n### The shape\r\n\r\nScout inline first â€” read `TICKETS.md`, pick the tickets for this cycle, group\r\nsame-file tickets into one item â€” then pass that list as `args` and let the\r\nscript fan out. `pipeline()` is the default: ticket B is being verified while\r\nticket C is still being fixed.\r\n\r\n```js\r\nexport const meta = {\r\n  name: 'work-tickets',\r\n  description: 'Fix and verify each open ticket, one agent per ticket',\r\n  phases: [{ title: 'Fix' }, { title: 'Verify' }],\r\n}\r\nconst VERDICT = { type: 'object', properties: {\r\n  id: {type: 'string'}, passed: {type: 'boolean'}, why: {type: 'string'} },\r\n  required: ['id', 'passed', 'why'] }\r\n\r\nconst results = await pipeline(\r\n  args,                                    // [{id: 'B3', title: 'â€¦'}, â€¦]\r\n  t => agent(`Fix ticket ${t.id}: ${t.title}. Report what you changed and how ` +\r\n             `you verified it. Note any unrelated problems you hit.`,\r\n             { label: `fix:${t.id}`, phase: 'Fix' }),\r\n  (fix, t) => agent(`Ticket ${t.id} â€” \"${t.title}\". An agent reports: ${fix}\r\n` +\r\n                    `Verify against the repo. Default to passed=false if unproven.`,\r\n             { label: `verify:${t.id}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT })\r\n)\r\nreturn results.filter(Boolean)\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Rules that don't bend\r\n\r\n- **The workflow never edits `TICKETS.md`.** Agents report; the main session\r\n  flips statuses after the call returns. Concurrent writes to the board is\r\n  exactly how tickets get lost.\r\n- **Flip to `[~]` before the call, to `[x]`/`[!]` after** â€” from the returned\r\n  verdicts, not from optimism. `passed: false` â†’ the ticket stays open, and its\r\n  `why` goes on the line or becomes a new `A` ticket.\r\n- **Same-file tickets are one item**, not two â€” group them when you build `args`,\r\n  or give them `isolation: 'worktree'` if they must run apart.\r\n- **Anything an agent finds along the way is an `A` ticket.** Ask for it in the\r\n  fix prompt, log the ones that come back.\r\n- **A `null` result means the agent died or was skipped.** `.filter(Boolean)`,\r\n  then leave that ticket `[~]` and say so â€” never silently drop it to done.\r\n- **Say what you dropped.** Capping the cycle at N tickets is fine; reporting it\r\n  as \"worked the board\" when 12 were open is not.\r\n\r\n## Fetching state\r\n\r\nWhole board: read `TICKETS.md`. Just the open ones:\r\n\r\n```\r\ngrep -nE '^\\s*- \\[[ ~!]\\]' TICKETS.md\r\n```\r\n\r\n`[x]` and `[-]` are the closed states, so neither shows up there; `[!]` does.\r\n\r\nWhen the user says a bare ID (\"do B3\", \"what's F12\"), grep for it and act on\r\nthat line.\r\n\r\n## Showing the board\r\n\r\nTwo views. **By type is the default** â€” `list tickets`, \"what's pending\", \"show\r\nthe board\", \"what's open\". **By status** when the user says so â€” `list tickets\r\nstatus`, \"status wise\", \"group by status\", \"what's in progress\".\r\n\r\nEither way: checkbox lines, the same shape the file uses. Never a prose summary,\r\nnever a markdown table, never a numbered list.\r\n\r\n### By type (default)\r\n\r\n```\r\nBugs\r\n  [~] B2 â€” CSV export drops the last row\r\n    [ ] B2b â€” add regression test\r\n  [!] B3 â€” Avatar upload 500s over 5MB (blocked: needs S3 creds from ops)\r\n\r\nFeatures\r\n  [ ] F1 â€” Dark mode\r\n  [ ] F2 â€” Bulk delete in the table view\r\n\r\nAgent-found\r\n  [ ] A1 â€” Unhandled promise rejection in the upload worker\r\n  [>] A2 â€” `parseDate` returns Invalid Date on empty string (deferred: after the parser rewrite)\r\n\r\n6 open (4 pending Â· 1 in-progress Â· 1 blocked) Â· 1 deferred Â· 9 closed (8 done Â· 1 cancelled) Â· 16 total\r\n```\r\n\r\n### By status (`list tickets status`)\r\n\r\nSame lines, regrouped. Sections in this order â€” **in-progress first**, because\r\nthat is what someone asking for a status view wants to see:\r\n\r\n```\r\nIn progress\r\n  [~] B2 â€” CSV export drops the last row\r\n\r\nBlocked\r\n  [!] B3 â€” Avatar upload 500s over 5MB (blocked: needs S3 creds from ops)\r\n\r\nDeferred\r\n  [>] A2 â€” `parseDate` returns Invalid Date on empty string (deferred: after the parser rewrite)\r\n\r\nPending\r\n  [ ] B2b â€” add regression test (B2)\r\n  [ ] F1 â€” Dark mode\r\n  [ ] F2 â€” Bulk delete in the table view\r\n  [ ] A1 â€” Unhandled promise rejection in the upload worker\r\n\r\n6 open (4 pending Â· 1 in-progress Â· 1 blocked) Â· 1 deferred Â· 9 closed (8 done Â· 1 cancelled) Â· 16 total\r\n```\r\n\r\nA sub-part shown away from its parent carries the parent ID in trailing\r\nparentheses â€” `(B2)` â€” since the indentation that explained it is gone. Include\r\n`Done` and `Cancelled` sections only when closed tickets were asked for.\r\n\r\n### Rules for both views\r\n\r\n- **Copy the line, don't rewrite it.** ID and title exactly as they appear in\r\n  `TICKETS.md`, so the user can grep for what they just read.\r\n- **Open and deferred, by default** (`[ ]`, `[~]`, `[!]`, `[>]`) â€” every\r\n  deferred ticket is listed, just counted apart. Closed tickets on request\r\n  (\"show everything\", \"what did we finish\") â€” then `[x]` and `[-]` too.\r\n- **Drop empty sections.** No \"Features: none\", no empty `Blocked`.\r\n- **Blocked and deferred show their reason**; that is the whole point of\r\n  `[!]` and `[>]`.\r\n- **The counts must add up, and the line must show its work.** Six mutually\r\n  exclusive states, two buckets and a loner:\r\n  `open = pending + in-progress + blocked` (blocked is open, just stuck) Â·\r\n  `closed = done + cancelled` Â· deferred is **neither** â€” it stands alone\r\n  between them, so it never inflates the open count someone is judging the\r\n  week by Â· `total = open + deferred + closed`. Each bucket prints its own\r\n  subtotal with the breakdown in parentheses, so the arithmetic is checkable on\r\n  sight and blocked is never mistaken for a bucket beside open.\r\n- **Always print all six states, zeros included.** Never drop a term because\r\n  it is `0`. `0 blocked` is information â€” it says nothing is stuck, which is\r\n  exactly what someone scanning the board wants to know â€” and a fixed-shape line\r\n  can be compared against last session's at a glance. An empty board still\r\n  prints the full line:\r\n\r\n  ```\r\n  0 open (0 pending Â· 0 in-progress Â· 0 blocked) Â· 0 deferred Â· 0 closed (0 done Â· 0 cancelled) Â· 0 total\r\n  ```\r\n- **One count line after the list**, and nothing else. No commentary on the\r\n  board's health, no suggested next ticket unless asked:\r\n\r\n  ```\r\n  6 open (4 pending Â· 1 in-progress Â· 1 blocked) Â· 1 deferred Â· 9 closed (8 done Â· 1 cancelled) Â· 16 total\r\n  ```\r\n- Legend only if the user seems new to the board, and then one line.\r\n\r\n## Rules\r\n\r\n- **One file, repo root, committed.** It travels with the branch; that is how\r\n  teammates and future chats see it.\r\n- **Append, never rewrite.** New tickets go at the end of their section; edits\r\n  touch only the one line being changed. Keeps merge conflicts to single lines.\r\n- **IDs are permanent.** No reuse, no renumbering, no deleting done tickets â€”\r\n  delete a ticket only if it was logged in error (say so to the user).\r\n- **Status reflects reality.** `[x]` means verified, not \"should work\". `[-]`\r\n  means deliberately closed without doing it â€” never use it to hide a ticket\r\n  that is still real, and `[!]` is not a parking space: it names what it waits on.\r\n---\r\nname: handoff-watch\r\ndescription: >-\r\n  Watch Claude Code usage limits and automatically write a session handoff the\r\n  moment usage crosses a threshold (97% by default), so a session that is about\r\n  to be cut off never loses its context. Use when the user asks to \"monitor usage\r\n  limits\", \"auto handoff before I run out\", \"warn me at 97%\", \"save the session\r\n  before the limit\", \"hand off automatically\", or wants to install/configure/debug\r\n  the usage watcher. This skill INSTALLS a statusline probe plus a Stop hook - the\r\n  monitoring itself is done by those hooks, not by the model. The handoff document\r\n  is written by the handoff skill, which the hook invokes. NOT for reporting\r\n  current usage (use /usage) and NOT for context-compaction tuning.\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Handoff Watch (auto-handoff at the usage limit)\r\n\r\nA skill cannot monitor anything - it is only text loaded into a turn. The watching\r\nhas to be done by the harness. This skill installs two small entry points into the\r\nsame script, then gets out of the way:\r\n\r\n| Piece | Event | Job |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `hooks/usage-watch.* probe` | `statusLine` | The **only** place Claude Code exposes `rate_limits.*.used_percentage`. Records the highest percentage seen to `~/.claude/handoff-watch/<session_id>`, then delegates to whatever statusline was already configured. |\r\n| `hooks/usage-watch.* gate` | `Stop` | At the end of a turn, if the recorded percentage â‰¥ threshold, returns `{\"decision\":\"block\"}` with the three-step preserve order below. |\r\n\r\nWhen it fires, Claude is told to do three things in order, so the saved state is\r\nwhole rather than just a narrative:\r\n\r\n1. **Drain the agents** â€” wait for or stop any running subagent, workflow or\r\n   background task, and record what it had accomplished. In-flight agent work is\r\n   never left undescribed.\r\n2. **Sync the board** â€” `skillator:ticket-master` over `TICKETS.md`, statuses only\r\n   (it is told *not* to start working open tickets â€” there is no budget left): close what\r\n   actually landed, downgrade half-done work to in-progress, file tickets for\r\n   anything found this session that has none.\r\n3. **Write the doc** â€” `skillator:handoff`, with a status table that matches\r\n   `TICKETS.md` ticket-for-ticket and a section listing the in-flight agent work\r\n   from step 1 plus the exact prompt to resume it.\r\n\r\nBoth `rate_limits` (5-hour and 7-day windows) and `context_window.used_percentage`\r\nare picked up; whichever is highest wins.\r\n\r\nFires **once per session** (a `.done` marker next to the flag file), and never\r\nloops (`stop_hook_active` is honoured).\r\n\r\n## Install\r\n\r\nUse **forward slashes** in every path â€” PowerShell accepts them and it removes\r\nall backslash-escaping from the JSON. Windows shown; on macOS/Linux use `usage-watch.sh` with `probe`/`gate` as\r\nthe first argument.\r\n\r\nAdd to `~/.claude/settings.json`:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"statusLine\": {\r\n    \"type\": \"command\",\r\n    \"command\": \"powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \\\"<SKILL>/hooks/usage-watch.ps1\\\" -Mode probe -Then \\\"<YOUR EXISTING STATUSLINE COMMAND>\\\"\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"hooks\": {\r\n    \"Stop\": [\r\n      { \"hooks\": [ { \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \\\"<SKILL>/hooks/usage-watch.ps1\\\" -Mode gate\", \"timeout\": 5 } ] }\r\n    ]\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `<SKILL>` is this skill's directory.\r\n- `-Then` is optional. Drop it if there is no existing statusline; the probe then\r\n  prints nothing and the statusline is empty.\r\n- POSIX: `\"command\": \"<SKILL>/hooks/usage-watch.sh probe '<YOUR EXISTING STATUSLINE COMMAND>'\"`.\r\n\r\n## Configure\r\n\r\n- **Threshold** â€” `CLAUDE_USAGE_HANDOFF_PCT` env var (default `97`). Set it in\r\n  `settings.json` under `env` to make it stick.\r\n- **Where the handoff lands** â€” decided by the `handoff` skill (`docs/handoffs/`\r\n  by default), not here.\r\n\r\n## Verify\r\n\r\n```\r\npowershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <SKILL>/hooks/selftest.ps1\r\n```\r\nPrints `ok`. It feeds fake statusline JSON through both modes and asserts: the\r\nmax percentage wins, no fire below threshold, a block with the percentage above\r\nit, no second fire, and no fire when `stop_hook_active`.\r\n\r\nTo check it live, temporarily set `CLAUDE_USAGE_HANDOFF_PCT=1` and end a turn -\r\nthe handoff should be written immediately.\r\n\r\n## Troubleshooting\r\n\r\n- **Never fires in one profile but works in another** â€” the wiring lives in\r\n  *that config dir's* `settings.json`. A `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` profile\r\n  (`~/.claude-work`, `~/.claude-bodish`, â€¦) is a separate install: arming\r\n  `~/.claude` arms nothing else. Wire every profile you actually code in, and\r\n  point `-Then` at that profile's own statusline command â€” the flag files are\r\n  shared (`~/.claude/handoff-watch/`) but the hooks are not.\r\n- **Never fires** â€” the statusline is where the percentage comes from. If\r\n  `~/.claude/handoff-watch/<session_id>` does not exist, the probe is not wired in\r\n  as `statusLine`, or this Claude Code build predates the `rate_limits` statusline\r\n  field (2.0.44+).\r\n- **Statusline went blank** â€” the `-Then` command is wrong or missing.\r\n- **Fired once, want it again** â€” delete `~/.claude/handoff-watch/<session_id>.done`.\r\n- **Stale flag files** â€” plain text, a few bytes each; delete\r\n  `~/.claude/handoff-watch/` whenever.\r\n\r\n## Related\r\n\r\n- `skillator:handoff` â€” writes the document this skill triggers.\r\n- `skillator:ticket-master` â€” the `TICKETS.md` board the handoff is reconciled against.\r\n- `skillator:handoff-resume` â€” executes it in the next session.\r\n\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
